s the year in  which Virginia Woolf also published her  breakthrough novel to the to the  lighthouse which is a woman painter the  third chapter from which I get the title  for the book is about Cezanne and  Einstein as the sort of on the one hand  the father of a paradigm shift in art  and the father of a paradigm shift in  science that weinstein never never  actually wrote about Cezanne in any way  and fourth chapter is about Cezanne and  the French philosopher merleau-ponty who  wrote extensively about about Cezanne  beginning in 1945 and then the final  chapter is concerned with Cezanne and  various figures in the psychoanalytic  and neuro neuroscience discipline from  the early 20th century on to the to the  present time okay and what led you to  write the book a number of things I had  done an exhibition on Cezanne and  written a book that went with it at the  at the getty back in 2004  I wasn't sure that I wanted to write a  monograph on Cezanne but but I did want  to come back in a kind of selective way  to to say sounds very groundbreaking  work  I wanted to extricate Cezanne from how  he's been normalized as as a sort of  father of abstraction and go back and  look at the and recover the strangeness  of seasons seasons work and then I also  wanted to use Cezanne to reconsider and  perhaps rethink the timelines of of 20th  century art into which he's been okay so  when you say the strangeness of his work  what do you mean well in in thinking  about Cezanne as a as the sort of father  of abstraction people have tended I  think to his to see his work as going in  the direction and leading sort of  inevitably to abstraction and so they  focused on a kind of 20th century  understanding of of Cezanne as really  interested in the sort of flatness of  the picture plane and and really  thinking in advance of abstraction and  that that's led to understanding him as  a kind of pre cubist pre abstract figure  and I think that's not adequate to to an  understanding of 